christoffel-symbol-of-the-second-kind-09.conceptual_definition:
The Christoffel symbol of the second kind, denoted $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho}$, specifies the components of the Levi-Civita affine connection $
abla$ in a coordinate basis $\{\partial_\mu\}$. It dictates how coordinate basis vectors change under parallel transport along coordinate directions via $
abla_{\partial_
u} \partial_ho = \Gamma^\mu_{
uho} \partial_\mu$, and is uniquely determined by first partial derivatives of the metric tensor: $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho} =  rac{1}{2} g^{\mu\sigma} (\partial_
u g_{ho\sigma} + \partial_ho g_{
u\sigma} - \partial_\sigma g_{
uho})$.

christoffel-symbol-of-the-second-kind-09.interpretation:
The symbol $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho}$ carries an upper contravariant index $\mu$ indicating the resulting component along the basis vector $\partial_\mu$, and lower covariant indices $
u$ and $ho$ denoting the direction of differentiation and the vector component being differentiated, respectively. Because the Levi-Civita connection is torsion-free, $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho}$ is symmetric in its lower indices: $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho} = \Gamma^\mu_{ho
u}$. While it does not transform as a tensor under general coordinate transformations due to an inhomogeneous gradient term $ rac{\partial x^\mu}{\partial x'^\alpha}  rac{\partial^2 x'^\alpha}{\partial x^
u \partial x^ho}$, it enables the construction of the gauge-covariant derivative $
abla_
u V^\mu = \partial_
u V^\mu + \Gamma^\mu_{
uho} V^ho$.

christoffel-symbol-of-the-second-kind-09.limits_and_boundary:
In flat Minkowski spacetime with global Cartesian coordinates where $g_{\mu
u} = \eta_{\mu
u}$, all derivatives vanish so $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho} = 0$. By the equivalence principle, at any single event $P$ in curved spacetime, one can always construct Local Inertial Coordinates (Riemann normal coordinates) such that $\Gamma^\mu_{
uho}(P) = 0$. In the Newtonian weak-field, slow-motion limit ($v \ll c$), the time-time-space component reduces to the gradient of the Newtonian potential: $\Gamma^i_{00} \approx  rac{1}{c^2}\partial_i \Phi$.

